The criterion C2.2 has a single Pass Threshold clause: “Unconditional provision covering 100% of basic needs (housing, food, healthcare, utilities) for all residents.” For a comprehensive system, “all residents” means the population the system’s own sources claim to serve (protocol 3.2). The source pack contains two conflicting statements about that population: the legislative text (integrated-implementation-roadmap.html, Section 102) says “Every citizen and legal resident 18 years or older shall receive 1,200 basic units monthly,” while the glossary and FAQ (wiki/glossary.html, wiki/faq.html) state Basic Units are “distributed universally to all residents regardless of work status.” This ambiguity about whether children and undocumented residents are covered is a genuine ambiguity in the system’s own sources. Per protocol 4.2, the ambiguity must be flagged; the preponderance of the detailed design text (the legislative draft) limits the guarantee to citizens/legal residents 18+, which is a subset of “all residents.” Even under the broader reading, the 1,200 BU/month (pegged 1:1 to USD) is stated to cover “essential needs” (corporate-transformation.html), but the BLEI calculation (basic-living-economic-index.html) shows an effective market purchasing power of $2,502/month only when BU are spent in PTF markets—i.e., the face value alone may not cover 100% of needs without the PTF price anchor. The provision is explicitly unconditional (no means testing, no work requirements; Section 102, wiki/faq.html), so the “unconditional” element is met. However, the population coverage and the 100%-of-needs coverage (at face value, without assuming PTF access) are not robustly shown for the full claimed population. Therefore the clause is not cleared.

The 0.0 condition (“no baseline security independent of labor market participation”) does not hold: the system explicitly provides a baseline independent of labor. Hence the score is 0.5.

